613 0 obj
<<
/Title (Trauma informed care \(TIC\) has emerged as a response to this concern, advocating for the systematic integration of trauma knowledge into all aspects of service delivery \(Fallot & Harris, 2009\). Core principles of TIC include safety, trustworthiness, choice, collaboration, and empowerment \(SAMHSA, 2014\), which closely align with voluntary and recovery oriented models of care. A growing body of evidence indicates that trauma-informed approaches are associated with improved engagement, reduced use of coercive practices, and decreased risk of retraumatisation across diverse settings, including inpatient psychiatric services, community mental health programs, and subs- -tance use recovery contexts \(Isobel & Edwards, 2015\). Importantly, TIC is increasingly conceptualised not merely as an individual clinician competency but as an organisa- -tional framework requiring systemic change to policies, environments, and power structures \(Menschner & Maul, 2016\). As such, trauma-informed practice is now widely regarded as foundational to ethical, person centred mental health care.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 548 0 R
/Next 647 0 R
>>
endobj
